The circumflex accent entered French orthography in the 16th–17th centuries as a composite of several earlier diacritic practices. Its well-documented uses include:
- marking the former presence of a deleted sibilant (H: forêt < forest, hôpital < hospital);
- signalling reduction of an earlier diphthong or hiatus (G/I: âge < aage, sûr < seur);
- distinguishing homophones that would otherwise be spelled identically (B: dû vs. du);
- indicating vowel-quality or length contrasts that had become phonemic after sound change (A/D: pâte vs. patte, mûr vs. mur).

Etymological or prestige spellings (F) are also attested, especially in the Renaissance when printers added circumflexes to give words a more “Latin” appearance.

